  ### 4) Architecture options (A vs B) with pros/cons and recommendation

  #### Option A: Custom Editor–based “Markdown Preview Editor” (recommended)

  Summary

  - Implement CustomTextEditorProvider for Markdown.
  - Own the preview webview + toolbar UI.
  - Apply edits to backing TextDocument via extension host edits.

  Pros

  - Full control of preview UX, toolbar, keyboard behavior, accessibility flow.
  - Deterministic edit semantics and message contracts.
  - No dependence on undocumented internals of built-in markdown preview.
  - Aligns with official custom editor guidance and sample patterns (Custom Editors guide (https://code.visualstudio.com/api/extension-guides/custom-
    editors), custom-editor sample (https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-extension-samples/blob/61fda64ede073136f3716f69b067d212ef0893e1/custom-editor-
    sample/src/catScratchEditor.ts)).

  Cons

  - Higher implementation cost (renderer, security hardening, selection mapping).
  - Requires explicit handling of webview security, CSP, and accessibility responsibilities.
  - Must maintain rendering parity with built-in markdown features over time.

  #### Option B: Extend/hijack built-in markdown preview

  Summary

  - Keep built-in preview surface and bolt editing commands around it.

  Pros

  - Lower initial implementation effort.
  - Maximum renderer parity with VS Code built-in preview.

  Cons

  - Limited control over preview internals; many behaviors are outside extension ownership.
  - Community evidence shows frequent edge-case friction with default preview-editor routing in workflows like diff/create-file.
  - Long-term fragility if relying on non-contract internals.
    (Markdown extension points scope (https://code.visualstudio.com/api/extension-guides/markdown-extension), Issue #191565 (https://github.com/
    microsoft/vscode/issues/191565), Issue #188338 (https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/188338), Issue #370 (https://github.com/yzhang-gh/
    vscode-markdown/issues/370)).

  Recommendation

  - Choose Option A.
  - Keep Option B fallback only as temporary migration path.

